> what is the gradient of x^2 y at (1, -2)
  ucas: it(x, y) = x^2*y
  ucas: grad it at 1, -2
∇ it = (2x*y, x^2)
  ∂/∂x it = 2x*y
  ∂/∂y it = x^2
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: every entry matches central differences at 200 points (max relative difference 4e-13)
  at (x = 1, y = -2): (-4, 1), |∇| = 4.123105626
> the hessian of x^2 y^3 at (-1, -2)
  ucas: it(x, y) = x^2*y^3
  ucas: hessian it at -1, -2
Hessian of it (row i, column j: ∂²/∂j∂i):
  [ 2y^3     6x*y^2 ]   ∂/∂x
  [ 6x*y^2   6x^2*y ]   ∂/∂y
  det = -24x^2*y^4
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: every entry matches central differences at 400 points (max relative difference 3e-13)
  symmetric (the mixed partials agree) at 100 of 100 sample pairs
  at (x = -1, y = -2):
  [ -16        -24        ]
  [ -24        -12        ]
  det = -384
  the gradient is not 0 there (largest component 16): not a critical point
> the directional derivative of x^2 y along (3, -4) at (1, 2)
  ucas: it(x, y) = x^2*y
  ucas: ddir it along 3, -4 at 1, 2
D_u it = 6x*y/5 - 4x^2/5
  u = v/|v| = (3/5, -4/5), |v| = 5  (the rate of change per unit length; `raw` for ∇it · v)
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: matches (f(p + h u) - f(p - h u))/2h at 100 points (max relative difference 1e-12)
  at (x = 1, y = 2): 8/5
> x squared, plus one
  ucas: it(x) = x^2 + 1
  ucas: show it
it(x: real) = x^2 + 1
  -> real
> wronskian sinh(x), cosh(x)
Wronskian matrix of sinh(x), cosh(x) (row k: the k-th derivatives wrt x)
  [ sinh(x)   cosh(x) ]   the functions
  [ cosh(x)   sinh(x) ]   d/dx
  W = det = -1
  (W equals the numeric determinant at all 200 sample points of [-10, 10])
  check: every entry matches central differences at 402 points (max relative difference 3e-15)
  linearly independent: W(-1/20) = -1 != 0, so the functions are independent on every interval containing x = -1/20
> wronskian sin x, cos x
Wronskian matrix of sin(x), cos(x) (row k: the k-th derivatives wrt x)
  [ sin(x)   cos(x)  ]   the functions
  [ cos(x)   -sin(x) ]   d/dx
  W = det = -1
  (W equals the numeric determinant at all 200 sample points of [-10, 10])
  check: every entry matches central differences at 402 points (max relative difference 3e-15)
  linearly independent: W(-1/20) = -1 != 0, so the functions are independent on every interval containing x = -1/20
> wronskian exp(x)*sin(x), exp(x)*cos(x)
Wronskian matrix of exp(x)*sin(x), exp(x)*cos(x) (row k: the k-th derivatives wrt x)
  [ exp(x)*sin(x)                   exp(x)*cos(x)                 ]   the functions
  [ sin(x)*exp(x) + cos(x)*exp(x)   cos(x)*exp(x) - sin(x)*exp(x) ]   d/dx
  W = det = -exp(x)^2
  W = -exp(2x)  (equal to the determinant at all 200 sample points of [-10, 10])
  check: every entry matches central differences at 402 points (max relative difference 8e-15)
  linearly independent: W(-1/20) = -0.904837418 != 0, so the functions are independent on every interval containing x = -1/20
> jacobian x*cos(y), x*sin(y) wrt x, y
Jacobian of (x*cos(y), x*sin(y)) wrt (x, y): row i = the gradient of function i
  [ cos(y)   -x*sin(y) ]
  [ sin(y)   x*cos(y)  ]
  det = x
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: every entry matches central differences at 400 points (max relative difference 3e-13)
> k(x, y) = log(cosh(x)*cos(y))
> hessian k
Hessian of k (row i, column j: ∂²/∂j∂i):
  [ 1/cosh(x)^2   0           ]   ∂/∂x
  [ 0             -1/cos(y)^2 ]   ∂/∂y
  det = -1/(cosh(x)^2*cos(y)^2)
  holds for cosh(x)*cos(y) > 0
  check: every entry matches central differences at 400 points (max relative difference 5e-07)
  symmetric (the mixed partials agree) at 100 of 100 sample pairs
> laplacian k
∇² k = 1/cosh(x)^2 - 1/cos(y)^2
  ∂²/∂x² k = 1/cosh(x)^2
  ∂²/∂y² k = -1/cos(y)^2
  holds for cosh(x)*cos(y) > 0
  check: every entry matches central differences at 400 points (max relative difference 5e-07)
> diff log(cosh(x)) x 2
d/dx log(cosh(x)) = sinh(x)/cosh(x)
d²/dx² log(cosh(x)) = 1/cosh(x)^2
  holds for cosh(x) > 0
  check: each order matches central differences at 402 points (max relative difference 2e-15)
